Not a behavioural divergence - a HARNESS one, and the reason is the stub std.

This fixture needs YOOP_STD_ROOT pointed at a stub carrying only `Display`,
because the point of it is that nothing imports the trait. The JS reference
lowers an interpolated template literal into a call to `stringConcatAll` and
refuses to emit one unless std/core/format.yoop and std/core/strings.yoop were
autoloaded - and a stub cannot supply those without dragging most of std into
the test tree. The bootstrap emits its own string builder inline and calls
libc, so it needs neither.

derive_display.yoop asserts the same expansion against BOTH compilers by
declaring `trait Display` in the fixture itself, which lets it run with the real
std discovered the ordinary way. What only this one covers is the BINDING.
